WEBVTT

00:00:00.221 --> 00:00:01.990
Last time ended with a ball rolling down

00:00:02.032 --> 00:00:04.001
a ramp and flying off it horizontally.

00:00:04.645 --> 00:00:05.449
Here's the experiment.

00:00:06.113 --> 00:00:08.297
The ramp's exit is ten centimetres above the

00:00:08.338 --> 00:00:11.445
bench, and the ball lands thirty-two centimetres away.

00:00:12.268 --> 00:00:14.857
Now raise the exit to forty centimetres, four

00:00:14.899 --> 00:00:16.866
times higher, and release the ball from the

00:00:16.907 --> 00:00:17.408
same spot.

00:00:18.154 --> 00:00:19.386
How much further will it land?

00:00:20.013 --> 00:00:22.520
The same, one and a half times, twice,

00:00:22.741 --> 00:00:23.944
or four times as far?

00:00:24.730 --> 00:00:25.112
Have a think.

00:00:28.989 --> 00:00:30.435
The model makes a sharp prediction.

00:00:31.098 --> 00:00:33.441
The ball leaves horizontally, so its fall time

00:00:33.562 --> 00:00:34.663
comes from the drop alone:

00:00:35.425 --> 00:00:37.668
h equals a half g t squared, so

00:00:37.749 --> 00:00:39.370
t is the square root of two h

00:00:39.552 --> 00:00:39.971
over g.

00:00:40.755 --> 00:00:42.869
Four times the height gives only twice the

00:00:42.910 --> 00:00:43.130
time.

00:00:43.795 --> 00:00:46.259
The horizontal velocity doesn't change, so the range,

00:00:46.661 --> 00:00:49.405
u x times t, is also twice, sixty-four

00:00:49.446 --> 00:00:49.926
centimetres.

00:00:50.608 --> 00:00:52.619
The model says R is proportional to the

00:00:52.680 --> 00:00:53.504
square root of h.

00:00:54.450 --> 00:00:56.618
So how can our own measurements test that

00:00:56.659 --> 00:00:57.039
prediction?

00:00:57.869 --> 00:01:00.502
Data you collect yourself, by measuring, is called

00:01:00.623 --> 00:01:01.366
primary data.

00:01:01.970 --> 00:01:03.433
To make it a fair test, only one

00:01:03.495 --> 00:01:04.577
thing should change at a time.

00:01:05.160 --> 00:01:07.347
Here we change the height, h, and measure

00:01:07.368 --> 00:01:08.169
the range, R.

00:01:08.753 --> 00:01:10.503
So the launch speed has to stay the

00:01:10.544 --> 00:01:11.449
same at every height.

00:01:12.053 --> 00:01:13.816
That means the same ball, released from the

00:01:13.877 --> 00:01:16.040
same marked point on the ramp, let go

00:01:16.142 --> 00:01:16.842
and never pushed.

00:01:17.424 --> 00:01:19.548
And the exit has to be horizontal, checked

00:01:19.569 --> 00:01:21.191
with a spirit level, so the ball leaves

00:01:21.232 --> 00:01:22.534
with no vertical velocity.

00:01:23.244 --> 00:01:24.298
Now measure carefully.

00:01:24.721 --> 00:01:27.989
Carbon paper laid over white paper marks exactly

00:01:28.030 --> 00:01:29.433
where the ball first touches down.

00:01:29.954 --> 00:01:32.499
A plumb line hanging from the exit marks

00:01:32.539 --> 00:01:34.883
the point directly below it, and R is

00:01:34.964 --> 00:01:36.888
measured from there to the centre of the

00:01:36.928 --> 00:01:37.188
mark.

00:01:37.869 --> 00:01:39.970
For the height, measure from the bench to

00:01:39.991 --> 00:01:41.631
the bottom of the ball at the exit,

00:01:42.032 --> 00:01:44.132
because that's how far the ball actually falls

00:01:44.273 --> 00:01:45.153
before it touches.

00:01:46.079 --> 00:01:47.989
Use at least five heights, and at least

00:01:48.070 --> 00:01:48.954
three trials at each.

00:01:50.045 --> 00:01:51.740
Before you start, a quick risk check.

00:01:52.163 --> 00:01:54.187
The ball bounces and rolls, so put a

00:01:54.247 --> 00:01:56.332
catch tray beyond the paper, where it can't

00:01:56.392 --> 00:01:58.317
roll off the bench and under someone's feet.

00:01:58.901 --> 00:02:00.916
Clamp the stand firmly so it can't tip.

00:02:01.359 --> 00:02:02.944
And if you use a spring launcher instead

00:02:02.985 --> 00:02:04.590
of a ramp, wear safety glasses.

00:02:05.581 --> 00:02:07.824
Here are three landing marks from one height,

00:02:08.105 --> 00:02:12.471
as illustrative values: fifty-three, fifty-five and fifty-seven centimetres.

00:02:13.173 --> 00:02:15.937
Identical launches don't land in exactly the same

00:02:15.977 --> 00:02:19.342
place, because tiny differences in release and spin

00:02:19.503 --> 00:02:20.504
change each flight.

00:02:21.045 --> 00:02:23.414
That scatter is random uncertainty.

00:02:23.997 --> 00:02:26.746
We report the mean, fifty-five centimetres, plus or

00:02:26.768 --> 00:02:29.015
minus half the spread, two centimetres.

00:02:29.658 --> 00:02:31.981
The rule's reading uncertainty is only half a

00:02:32.022 --> 00:02:34.546
millimetre, so here it's the scatter, not the

00:02:34.607 --> 00:02:35.708
ruler, that limits us.

00:02:36.471 --> 00:02:39.140
Repeating trials makes the mean more reliable, but

00:02:39.161 --> 00:02:41.610
it doesn't make any single landing less scattered.

00:02:42.279 --> 00:02:43.252
Now the full set.

00:02:43.837 --> 00:02:46.628
These are sample values, supplied to practise processing,

00:02:46.970 --> 00:02:48.315
not anyone's own results.

00:02:49.101 --> 00:02:51.636
Plot R against h, and the points curve.

00:02:52.160 --> 00:02:53.815
A curve is hard to judge by eye.

00:02:54.320 --> 00:02:56.202
But if R is proportional to the square

00:02:56.283 --> 00:02:58.907
root of h, then plotting R against root

00:02:59.148 --> 00:03:00.970
h should give a straight line through the

00:03:01.111 --> 00:03:01.391
origin.

00:03:02.173 --> 00:03:04.223
Choosing what to plot so the model predicts

00:03:04.244 --> 00:03:06.394
a straight line is called linearising.

00:03:06.917 --> 00:03:08.764
And here, the points do line up.

00:03:09.498 --> 00:03:10.769
The gradient has a meaning.

00:03:11.436 --> 00:03:11.937
From the model,

00:03:12.580 --> 00:03:14.909
R equals u x times root two over

00:03:14.930 --> 00:03:16.274
g, times root h.

00:03:16.737 --> 00:03:19.012
So the gradient is u x times root

00:03:19.053 --> 00:03:19.676
two over g.

00:03:20.420 --> 00:03:21.861
The line of best fit here has a

00:03:21.903 --> 00:03:24.466
gradient of about one point zero, in units

00:03:24.507 --> 00:03:26.991
of root metres, and it passes almost through

00:03:27.032 --> 00:03:27.432
the origin.

00:03:28.134 --> 00:03:28.694
Rearranging,

00:03:29.256 --> 00:03:31.519
u x equals the gradient times root g

00:03:31.600 --> 00:03:33.783
over two, about two point two metres per

00:03:33.825 --> 00:03:34.104
second.

00:03:34.868 --> 00:03:36.362
Now here's the real test.

00:03:36.888 --> 00:03:39.020
Measure u x another way, say with a

00:03:39.081 --> 00:03:40.026
light gate at the exit.

00:03:40.570 --> 00:03:42.979
If the two values agree within their uncertainties,

00:03:43.321 --> 00:03:45.429
the model has passed a genuine test.

00:03:46.364 --> 00:03:47.818
But what if they don't agree?

00:03:48.622 --> 00:03:51.711
Imagine repeated landings spread over about six centimetres,

00:03:52.192 --> 00:03:54.317
while the mean lands about four centimetres short

00:03:54.358 --> 00:03:55.701
of the prediction, every time.

00:03:56.505 --> 00:03:57.319
The spread is random.

00:03:57.744 --> 00:03:59.930
It sets the uncertainty, about plus or minus

00:03:59.970 --> 00:04:00.692
three centimetres.

00:04:01.554 --> 00:04:04.259
The shortfall is a systematic error, because it

00:04:04.319 --> 00:04:07.043
shifts every result the same way, and averaging

00:04:07.083 --> 00:04:08.666
more trials will never remove it.

00:04:09.550 --> 00:04:10.575
Air resistance could cause it.

00:04:11.058 --> 00:04:13.502
So could an exit tilted slightly down, or

00:04:13.543 --> 00:04:14.865
a height measured to the centre of the

00:04:14.906 --> 00:04:15.867
ball instead of the bottom.

00:04:16.491 --> 00:04:18.164
Each is a lead to investigate.

00:04:19.158 --> 00:04:19.762
One more warning.

00:04:20.326 --> 00:04:22.853
A straight line through the origin doesn't prove

00:04:22.894 --> 00:04:24.358
there's no systematic error.

00:04:24.980 --> 00:04:26.743
If the ball always left a little slower

00:04:26.764 --> 00:04:29.247
than you assumed, every range would shrink by

00:04:29.288 --> 00:04:31.291
the same fraction, and the graph would still

00:04:31.332 --> 00:04:33.115
be a perfect straight line through the origin,

00:04:33.516 --> 00:04:34.718
just with a lower gradient.

00:04:35.402 --> 00:04:37.554
That's why the check against an independent u

00:04:37.716 --> 00:04:38.198
x matters.

00:04:38.802 --> 00:04:40.807
And even a good match only supports the

00:04:40.848 --> 00:04:43.054
model over the heights and speeds you actually

00:04:43.095 --> 00:04:43.455
tested.

00:04:44.344 --> 00:04:45.940
So keep two ideas apart.

00:04:46.424 --> 00:04:49.050
Spread is random, and a shift is systematic.

00:04:49.612 --> 00:04:51.856
Repeats steady the mean, not the shift.

00:04:54.502 --> 00:04:54.925
Your turn.

00:04:55.871 --> 00:05:00.155
In a horizontal-launch investigation, three repeated ranges from

00:05:00.335 --> 00:05:03.297
one height are zero point seven zero, zero

00:05:03.317 --> 00:05:05.519
point seven two and zero point seven four

00:05:05.579 --> 00:05:05.919
metres.

00:05:06.600 --> 00:05:09.858
Calculate the mean range and its uncertainty as

00:05:09.958 --> 00:05:10.682
half the spread.

00:05:11.165 --> 00:05:13.833
Then explain whether the repeated trials show that

00:05:13.874 --> 00:05:15.117
the result is accurate.

00:05:15.722 --> 00:05:16.854
Pause here to answer.

00:05:27.640 --> 00:05:30.184
The mean is zero point seven two metres,

00:05:30.805 --> 00:05:32.969
and half the spread is zero point seven

00:05:33.049 --> 00:05:36.134
four minus zero point seven zero, over two,

00:05:36.434 --> 00:05:38.037
which is zero point zero two.

00:05:38.759 --> 00:05:41.690
So R is zero point seven two, plus

00:05:41.730 --> 00:05:43.717
or minus zero point zero two metres.

00:05:44.783 --> 00:05:47.597
Repeats show the result is precise, not that

00:05:47.618 --> 00:05:48.321
it's accurate.

00:05:48.965 --> 00:05:51.133
A tilted exit or a mis-measured height would

00:05:51.174 --> 00:05:53.743
shift all three trials together, and the spread

00:05:53.764 --> 00:05:54.465
would never show it.

00:05:55.189 --> 00:05:57.197
The slip is saying more trials make it

00:05:57.358 --> 00:05:57.719
accurate.

00:05:59.252 --> 00:05:59.678
Last one.

00:06:00.368 --> 00:06:03.030
A student plots range R against the square

00:06:03.071 --> 00:06:05.292
root of the drop height h, and the

00:06:05.313 --> 00:06:07.113
line of best fit has a gradient of

00:06:07.395 --> 00:06:09.195
one point zero zero root metres.

00:06:10.038 --> 00:06:12.783
Using R equals u x times the square

00:06:12.824 --> 00:06:14.327
root of two h over g,

00:06:14.869 --> 00:06:16.813
calculate the horizontal launch speed.

00:06:17.697 --> 00:06:18.523
Pause here to answer.

00:06:29.128 --> 00:06:30.190
The gradient is u

00:06:30.733 --> 00:06:32.918
x times root two over g.

00:06:33.620 --> 00:06:35.641
So u x is the gradient times root

00:06:35.742 --> 00:06:36.642
g over two,

00:06:37.303 --> 00:06:39.704
one point zero zero times root four point

00:06:39.765 --> 00:06:42.366
nine, about two point two one metres per

00:06:42.407 --> 00:06:42.707
second.

00:06:43.650 --> 00:06:46.097
The slip is treating the gradient itself as

00:06:46.138 --> 00:06:46.599
the speed.

00:06:47.323 --> 00:06:49.898
Its unit is root metres, so it isn't

00:06:49.920 --> 00:06:50.220
a speed.

00:06:52.329 --> 00:06:54.696
So far, every force has changed how something

00:06:54.797 --> 00:06:55.960
moves along a path.

00:06:56.704 --> 00:06:58.832
Next, forces that make things turn.

00:06:59.557 --> 00:07:01.272
Here's a spanner on a stuck nut.

00:07:01.858 --> 00:07:03.379
Push on the end of the handle with

00:07:03.400 --> 00:07:05.741
the same force, in three different directions.

00:07:06.544 --> 00:07:08.632
One turns the nut best, and one does

00:07:08.733 --> 00:07:09.294
nothing at all.

00:07:10.021 --> 00:07:10.243
Why?

00:07:12.995 --> 00:07:13.817
Here are the key notes.

00:07:14.341 --> 00:07:15.888
Pause the video if you need more time

00:07:15.929 --> 00:07:16.350
to copy them.
